<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="82fca2e2">&lt;p&gt;
    Data architecture can be represented in conceptual, logical, or physical forms.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Conceptual level:&lt;/strong&gt; describes the semantics of a domain (such as customer or account data) and is
    essentially a set of technology independent specifications about the data used to discuss initial requirements with the
    business stakeholders. This information is also used by data modelers to define one or more logical data
    models. It consists of entity classes, representing information of significance in the domain, and relationship
    assertions about associations between pairs of entity classes.   
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Logical level:&lt;/strong&gt; represents the abstract structure of a domain of information. They are often
    diagrammatic in nature and are used to capture things of importance to an organization and how they relate to one
    another. Once validated and approved, the logical data architecture can become the basis of a physical data
    architecture and inform the design of data repositories.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Physical level&lt;/strong&gt;: is a representation of a data design which takes into account the facilities and
    constraints of a given database management system, which accounts for data access, performance, and storage. A complete
    physical data architecture will include all the database artifacts required to create relationships between tables
    or to achieve performance goals, such as indexes, constraint definitions, linking tables, partitioned tables or
    clusters.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="externalId" nodeId="a582c676"></attribute>
<attribute name="keyConsiderations" nodeId="f1084cf8"></attribute>
<attribute name="longPresentationName" nodeId="c6abff34"></attribute>
<attribute name="purpose" nodeId="b5f9474e">&lt;p&gt;
    The purpose of this artifact is to:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Illustrate the data entity types, attributes, relationships, integrity rules and the definitions of these objects
        to business and IT stakeholders of an organization that make business decisions and build information systems,
        respectively.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Address the stakeholder concerns related to data.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Help identify candidate architecture roadmap components based upon gaps between the baseline and target
        architectures.
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="impactOfNotHaving" nodeId="25001b96"></attribute>
<attribute name="reasonsForNotNeeding" nodeId="244a01fa"></attribute>
<attribute name="briefOutline" nodeId="641bdbd">&lt;p&gt;
    Data architecture generally contains the following components:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Data model:&lt;/strong&gt; This will normally consist of entity types, attributes, relationships, integrity rules,
    and the definitions of those objects. This is then used as the start point for interface or database design.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Data sources:&lt;/strong&gt; These include system of records (SORs), reference data stores, master-data data
    stores and others that host data within an enterprise.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Operational Data Store (ODS):&lt;/strong&gt; This is designed to integrate data from multiple sources and perform
    additional operations on data such as data sorting, duplicate checks, etc.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Data marts and warehouses:&lt;/strong&gt; These include a series of databases typically with large storage that
    receive data feeds from various data sources across an organization. Reporting and analytics are typically performed
    based on the data from these warehouses.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Data integration components:&lt;/strong&gt; These include specific components such as Extract, Transform, and Load
    (ETL) that facilitate data extraction, manipulation, and transportation across the data integration architecture
    landscape.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Data integration patterns&lt;/strong&gt;: These include patterns such as hub and spoke architecture to adopt a
    standardized and leading industry-based integration approach that a data architecture solution can benefit
    from.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
